# The endpoints the file browser talks to (#155). Every request below has been run against
# a real dufs behind the agent's gateway, and what came back is recorded under it.
#
# The gateway's certificate is signed by the device key, and no authority signs that, so an
# HTTP client has to be told not to check it. `http-client.env.json` next to this file does
# that for Android Studio and IntelliJ. The Kotlin client pins on the certificate key
# instead, which is what #127 proved.
#
# What this was run against:
#
#   agent share add <a directory> --name files
#   agent run
#
# The same requests with curl, for a terminal:
#
#   curl -sk 'https://127.0.0.1:7433/files/?json'

@gateway = 127.0.0.1:7433
@app = files

### The listing. This is the screen.
GET https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/?json

# 200 application/json.
#
# {
#   "href": "/",
#   "kind": "Index",
#   "uri_prefix": "/files/",
#   "allow_upload": true,
#   "allow_delete": true,
#   "allow_search": true,
#   "allow_archive": true,
#   "dir_exists": true,
#   "auth": false,
#   "user": null,
#   "paths": [
#     { "path_type": "Dir",  "name": "sub",       "mtime": 1789834173828, "size": 1 },
#     { "path_type": "File", "name": "blob.bin",  "mtime": 1789834173826, "size": 1048576 },
#     { "path_type": "File", "name": "notes.txt", "mtime": 1789834173822, "size": 26 }
#   ]
# }
#
# path_type is "Dir" or "File" and is the only thing that says which. mtime is in
# milliseconds. A directory's size is how many entries it holds, not bytes.
# allow_upload and allow_delete say which buttons belong on the screen.

### A directory below the top one. Walking down is the same request with the path on it.
GET https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/sub/?json

# 200, the same shape, with "href": "/sub" and uri_prefix still "/files/". Neither is
# needed to build the next request: a row's path is the directory the client asked for
# plus the row's name.

### Download a file.
GET https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/notes.txt

# 200, the bytes.
#
# Accept-Ranges: bytes
# Content-Disposition: inline; filename="notes.txt"
# Content-Length: 26
# Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
# Etag: "1789834173822-26"
# Last-Modified: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 16:09:33 GMT

### Part of a file. A download that has to resume starts here.
GET https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/blob.bin
Range: bytes=0-99

# 206, 100 bytes.

### Upload. The name in the path is the name it lands under.
PUT https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/uploaded.txt
Content-Type: text/plain

uploaded by the collection

# 201, empty body. There is no multipart form and no separate create call.
# A path whose directories do not exist yet is made on the way: PUT of
# /files/newdir/deep.txt answered 201 and newdir was there afterwards.

### Delete. The same path, and a directory goes the same way.
DELETE https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/uploaded.txt

# 204, empty body. DELETE of a directory also answered 204 and took its contents with it.

### A name with spaces and non-ASCII in it, percent-encoded.
PUT https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/a%20b%20%C3%A9.txt
Content-Type: text/plain

spaces and an accent

# 201. The listing gives the name decoded, "a b é.txt", so the client encodes each path
# segment on the way back out. GET and DELETE of the encoded path answered 200 and 204.

### A file that is not there.
GET https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/nope.txt

# 404.

### An application this PC does not share.
GET https://{{gateway}}/nosuchapp/?json

# 404, from the gateway rather than from dufs.

### ?json on a file rather than a directory.
GET https://{{gateway}}/{{app}}/notes.txt?json

# 200, and it describes the file instead of listing anything:
# { "path_type": "File", "name": "notes.txt", "mtime": ..., "size": 26 }
# The browser does not need it. The parent listing already said which entries are
# directories.

### The discovery document, for reference. The client reads this first and it is what
### proves which certificate key the requests above have to be pinned to (#127).
GET https://{{gateway}}/.well-known/anywhere-file

# 200, with device_id, name, apps, public_key and tls_proof.
